      "text": "Given the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0\\) with roots \\(a, b, c\\), we use Vieta's formulas to determine the relationships between the coefficients and the roots:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = -3, \\quad ab + bc + ca = 4, \\quad abc = 11.\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider the transformed polynomial \\(x^3 + rx^2 + sx + t = 0\\) with roots \\(a+b, b+c, c+a\\). We use Vieta's formulas again to find \\(a+b, b+c, c+a\\):\n\n1. **Sum of the roots \\(a + b + c\\) for \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0\\):**\n\\[\na + b + c = -3.\n\\]\n\n2. **Sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time for \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0\\):**\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = 4.\n\\]\n\n3. **The constant term (product of the roots for the original polynomial) for \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0\\):**\n\\[\nabc = -11,\n\\]\nwhich corresponds to the coefficient \\(t\\) in the new polynomial, thus \\(t = -11\\).\n\nFinally, we verify if the same relationships hold for the new polynomial:\n\n\\[\na+b + b+c + c+a = (a+b) + (b+c) + (c+a) = 2(a+b+c) = 2(-3) = -6.\n\\]\n\nGiven this, in the transformed polynomial \\(x^3 + rx^2 + sx + t = 0\\), using Vieta's formulas, \\(a + b + c\\) corresponds to:\n\n\\[\nr = -6.\n\\]\n\nCalculate the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = 4.\n\\]\n\nThe constant term \\(t\\) corresponds to:\n\n\\[\nabc = 11.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final value for \\(t\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11}.\n\\]",
